  *Academic notice.* This handbook is a master's-level teaching text in counselling psychology and the behavioural sciences, issued to support the lecture courses of the Master in Children and Adolescent Counseling. It is written for study, examination, and professional education. It is not clinical supervision, diagnosis, or treatment, and it does not by itself confer a master's or any other academic qualification. Award of the degree depends on enrolment, satisfactory completion of the courses listed in the programme overview, supervised practice where required, and successful examination by the University.

  *Professional notice.* Nothing in this handbook authorises or supports practice beyond the reader's training, competence, licensure, and lawful scope of practice, or practice that is contrary to the ethical codes and legal requirements of the jurisdiction concerned. Work with children and adolescents is regulated; duties of reporting, consent, confidentiality, and record-keeping are creatures of local law and of the reader's employing institution. Readers must obtain the current codes, statutes, and standards referred to in these pages. The law and professional regulation described are stated as understood at the date of writing (2026) and are given as a framework for study, not as legal advice.

  *Safeguarding notice.* Where this handbook describes risk of harm to a child or young person, the material is teaching material. A reader who encounters actual or suspected abuse, neglect, exploitation, or risk of serious harm must follow the safeguarding and reporting procedure of their jurisdiction, employer, or placement, immediately, and must not rely on this text in place of that procedure.

  *Research notice.* Studies, effect sizes, and findings are reported in summary form to support teaching and analysis. Readers who rely on a particular finding must consult the original publication, its methods, its sample, and any subsequent replication or critique. Citations follow the seventh edition of the *Publication Manual of the American Psychological Association*; works are listed only where they are actually cited.

  *Hypothetical material.* Cases, children, families, schools, and scenarios marked as hypothetical or as fictitious composites are invented and are used exclusively for teaching and examination purposes. Ages, names, and identifying detail have been altered. Any resemblance to actual persons is coincidental. Where case material derives from a published source, the source is cited. No real client, patient, pupil, or student material appears in this handbook.

  *Illustrations.* Diagrams and figures were prepared for this edition. Photographs marked "Illustrative image (AI-generated)" were created for teaching purposes and do not depict any identifiable person, family, school, clinic, or agency.

  *Accessibility.* This file is exported as a tagged PDF conforming to PDF/UA-1, with a document structure tree, bookmark navigation to heading level three, language set to British English, and alternative text on informative figures. Text and background combinations meet WCAG 2.2 Level AA contrast (4.5:1). Readers requiring an alternative format should contact the University's student support office.

  Typeset in EB Garamond and Source Sans 3, both licensed under the SIL Open Font License 1.1. Trim size 6 × 9 inches.
